VALUES

(
  'Cap Fund Academy: Who We Are and What We Teach',
  'cap-fund-academy-about',
  'microlending-basics',
  'Cap Fund Academy is an independent certification platform built for rural nonprofits, CDFIs, and community lenders who want to master USDA rural capital programs. Here is exactly what we teach and who we serve.',
  E'## What Is Cap Fund Academy?\n\nCap Fund Academy (capfundacademy.com) is an independent online certification platform. We train rural nonprofits, Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs), tribal economic development organizations, community development corporations, county agencies, and mission-driven lenders to build, fund, launch, manage, and sustain microlending and revolving loan fund programs.\n\n**We are not affiliated with USDA or any government agency.** We are an independent training company staffed by practitioners who have worked directly with rural capital programs.\n\n## What We Teach\n\nOur 17 stackable certifications cover every aspect of rural capital access:\n\n- **USDA RMAP** — The Rural Microentrepreneur Assistance Program (7 CFR 4280 Subpart D): eligibility, application, 125-point scoring rubric, RMRF/LLRF account management, TA&T programs, annual reporting, and USDA site visits\n- **USDA RBDG** — The Rural Business Development Grant (7 CFR 4280 Subpart E): RLF establishment, project types, scoring, leverage, and post-award compliance\n- **IRP** — The Intermediary Relending Program (7 U.S.C. 1932(b)): 1% fixed-rate loans to eligible intermediaries for rural business relending\n- **Revolving Loan Funds** — Fund design, capitalization, governance, loan committee structure, written loan policy, underwriting, servicing, and compliance\n- **Federal Compliance** — 2 CFR 200 Uniform Guidance, civil rights (ECOA, Title VI, ADA, Section 504), NEPA environmental review, SAM.gov/UEI, debarment\n- **RLF Accounting** — Restricted fund accounting, RMRF/LLRF controls, program income, USDA reporting, SF-270 drawdowns, single audit preparation\n- **Application Assembly** — Evidence crosswalk methodology, reviewer-centered packet assembly, narrative strengthening, AI scoring\n\n## Who Should Get Certified\n\nCap Fund Academy certifications are designed for:\n\n- Executive directors and program staff at rural nonprofits preparing USDA applications\n- Loan officers and program directors at CDFIs and community lenders\n- Tribal economic development staff\n- County and regional economic development agency staff\n- Grant writers and consultants supporting rural capital access clients\n- Anyone building or operating a revolving loan fund\n\n## Our Credential Ladder\n\n- **Rural Microfinance Associate** (Certs 1–4) — $1,497\n- **Revolving Loan Fund Practitioner** (Certs 1–9) — $2,997\n- **USDA Rural Capital Program Specialist** (Certs 1–13) — $3,997\n- **Certified RLF Executive** (Certs 1–15) — $4,997\n- **Master Rural Microfinance & RLF Administrator** (All 17) — $5,997\n\n## How Certifications Work\n\nEach certification is self-paced, delivered entirely online. Students complete lessons, take a 15-question quiz (80% passing score), and submit a required artifact — a practical document or plan demonstrating mastery of the material. Our AI Study Coach (powered by GPT-4o) answers questions 24/7. Audio lessons are available for every certification.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform. Training does not guarantee USDA funding, eligibility, or approval.*',
  'About Cap Fund Academy | Rural Capital Access Certification',
  'Cap Fund Academy is an independent certification platform training rural nonprofits, CDFIs, and community lenders in USDA RMAP, RBDG, IRP, and revolving loan fund operations.',
  true, 'published', now() - interval '2 days'
),

(
  'How to Start a Microloan Program: Step-by-Step Guide for Rural Nonprofits',
  'how-to-start-microloan-program-rural-nonprofits',
  'microlending-basics',
  'Starting a rural microloan program requires organizational structure, a written loan policy, capitalization, and USDA program eligibility. Here is the complete step-by-step process.',
  E'## Can Your Organization Start a Microloan Program?\n\nYes — if your organization is a nonprofit, public agency, or tribal entity with a rural service area and a mission aligned with economic development or microenterprise development, you may be eligible to operate a federally funded rural microloan program under USDA''s RMAP or RBDG programs.\n\nThis guide walks through every step.\n\n## Step 1: Confirm Eligibility\n\nFor USDA RMAP, you need to be:\n- A private nonprofit, public agency, or Indian tribe\n- Serving a primarily rural area (USDA-designated)\n- Have a mission of economic development or microenterprise development\n- Have an active SAM.gov registration with a current UEI\n\nFor RBDG, eligibility is similar but also includes rural public bodies (counties, municipalities).\n\n## Step 2: Build Your Organizational Infrastructure\n\nBefore applying for federal capitalization, you need:\n\n**Board governance:**\n- A board with relevant experience (lending, finance, community development)\n- Regular board meetings with documented minutes\n- A loan committee with clear authority and documented procedures\n\n**Written loan policy:**\nUSDA requires a complete written loan fund policy covering eligible borrowers, loan terms, underwriting standards, application process, closing procedures, collections, conflict of interest, and account management. This is the single most common gap in RMAP applications.\n\n**Bank accounts:**\n- A dedicated revolving fund account (RMRF for RMAP funds)\n- A loan loss reserve account (LLRF) for RMAP programs\n- Never commingled with general operating funds\n\n## Step 3: Capitalize Your Fund\n\nMost organizations follow this sequence:\n\n1. **Apply for RBDG** — USDA Rural Business Development Grant provides grants (no repayment) to establish the RLF and demonstrate initial lending capability\n2. **Build a track record** — Make at least a few microloans under the RBDG-funded RLF\n3. **Apply for RMAP** — With 1-3 years of microlending history, apply for RMAP to scale the program with up to $500,000 in revolving loan capital\n4. **Layer in IRP** — Once capacity is proven, the Intermediary Relending Program provides 1% fixed-rate loans for 30 years to further capitalize the RLF\n\n## Step 4: Design Your Loan Products\n\nDefine your loan products before making your first loan:\n- Minimum and maximum loan amounts\n- Interest rate range\n- Maximum term (RMAP: 10 years)\n- Eligible purposes\n- Collateral requirements\n- Credit score policy\n\n## Step 5: Build Your TA Program\n\nUSDA RMAP requires approved microlenders to provide technical assistance to microentrepreneurs. Your TA program must be:\n- Documented (session logs, attendees, topics, outcomes)\n- Aligned with borrower success (business planning, financial management, loan readiness)\n- Measurable (track TA hours, businesses assisted, repayment outcomes)\n\n## Step 6: Apply and Get Approved\n\nThe RMAP application is scored on 125 points. The most competitive applications have:\n- A complete, current written loan policy\n- 3 years of documented microlending history\n- Strong TA documentation with outcome data\n- Multiple letters of commitment from SBDCs, banks, and partners\n- Current audited financial statements\n\n*Cap Fund Academy''s Cert 3 covers the complete RMAP scoring rubric and evidence documentation strategy.*',
  'How to Start a Microloan Program | Rural Nonprofits Guide | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Step-by-step guide for rural nonprofits to start a USDA-funded microloan program: eligibility, loan policy, RBDG capitalization, RMAP application, and TA program requirements.',
  true, 'published', now() - interval '4 days'
),

(
  'USDA RMAP vs SBA Microloan: Which Program Is Right for Your Organization?',
  'usda-rmap-vs-sba-microloan-comparison',
  'rmap-readiness',
  'USDA RMAP and the SBA Microloan Program both fund rural microlenders — but the eligibility rules, loan limits, TA requirements, and application processes are very different. Here is how to choose.',
  E'## Two Federal Microloan Programs — Different Tools for Different Organizations\n\nBoth USDA RMAP and the SBA Microloan Program provide capital to nonprofit intermediaries for microlending to small businesses. But they operate differently, serve different geographic markets, and have different competitive dynamics.\n\n## USDA RMAP\n\n**Who it funds:** Microenterprise Development Organizations (MDOs) — nonprofits, public agencies, and tribes with a rural service area\n\n**Loan to intermediary:** Direct loan from USDA, up to $500,000 per application cycle. Repayment required.\n\n**Maximum microloan to borrower:** $50,000\n\n**Borrower eligibility:** Rural microenterprises — businesses with 10 or fewer FTEs located in a rural area\n\n**TA requirement:** Mandatory — approved MDOs must provide documented technical assistance to microentrepreneurs\n\n**Competitive dynamics:** National competition, quarterly review, 125-point scoring rubric\n\n**Best for:** Rural-focused organizations with a mission aligned to microenterprise development in rural areas\n\n## SBA Microloan Program\n\n**Who it funds:** Nonprofit intermediary lenders designated by SBA\n\n**Loan to intermediary:** SBA loans funds to intermediaries at approximately 1.25% above the 5-year Treasury rate. Repayment required.\n\n**Maximum microloan to borrower:** $50,000 (same cap)\n\n**Borrower eligibility:** Small businesses and nonprofits — not limited to rural areas\n\n**TA requirement:** Intermediaries must provide technical assistance but requirements are less prescriptive than RMAP\n\n**Competitive dynamics:** SBA designates intermediaries through a separate process\n\n**Best for:** Urban and suburban-focused organizations or those serving both rural and non-rural markets\n\n## Key Differences\n\n| Factor | USDA RMAP | SBA Microloan |\n|--------|-----------|---------------|\n| Geographic focus | Rural required | Any geography |\n| Federal instrument | Direct loan | SBA loan |\n| Rate to intermediary | Fixed (see 7 CFR) | ~1.25% + 5yr Treasury |\n| TA requirements | Mandatory, documented | Required, less prescriptive |\n| Borrower cap | 10 FTEs | Not specified |\n| Geographic area req. | Rural designation | Not required |\n\n## Which Should You Pursue?\n\nFor rural-focused organizations, RMAP is typically the better fit because:\n1. The program is specifically designed for rural microenterprise development\n2. The scoring rewards rural service area documentation\n3. The TA requirement creates a structure that improves loan performance\n4. You can layer RMAP with RBDG and IRP to build a larger capital stack\n\nSome organizations pursue both — using RMAP for rural borrowers and SBA for urban borrowers in their service area.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy covers USDA RMAP in depth in Certifications 3-5. Training is educational and does not guarantee eligibility for any federal program.*',
  'USDA RMAP vs SBA Microloan Program | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Compare USDA RMAP and the SBA Microloan Program: eligibility, loan limits, TA requirements, geographic focus, and which program is right for your rural nonprofit.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '6 days'
),

(
  'What Is a CDFI? How Community Development Financial Institutions Work',
  'what-is-cdfi-community-development-financial-institutions',
  'microlending-basics',
  'Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) are certified by the U.S. Treasury to provide financial services to underserved communities. Here is how CDFIs work, how to get certified, and how CDFI certification relates to USDA rural capital programs.',
  E'## What Is a CDFI?\n\nA Community Development Financial Institution (CDFI) is an organization certified by the U.S. Department of the Treasury''s CDFI Fund as having a primary mission of promoting community development by providing financial products and services to underserved communities and populations.\n\nCDFIs include:\n- Community development banks\n- Community development credit unions\n- Community development loan funds\n- Community development venture capital funds\n- Microenterprise development funds\n\n## How CDFI Certification Works\n\nCDFI certification is administered by the CDFI Fund (Treasury). To become certified, an organization must demonstrate:\n\n1. **Primary mission** — Community development is the primary purpose\n2. **Financing entity** — The organization primarily provides loans, investments, or financial services\n3. **Target market** — At least 60% of activities serve a target market of underserved people or communities\n4. **Accountability** — Formal accountability to the target market through governance\n5. **Non-government** — Not a government agency\n\nCDFI certification takes 3-12 months and must be maintained through annual reporting.\n\n## CDFI Fund Awards\n\nCertified CDFIs can apply for CDFI Fund awards:\n\n- **Financial Assistance (FA) awards** — Up to $2M per cycle for lending capital and operations\n- **Technical Assistance (TA) grants** — Smaller grants for emerging CDFIs\n- **CDFI Bond Guarantee Program** — Long-term, fixed-rate bond financing for larger CDFIs\n\n## How CDFI Certification Relates to USDA Programs\n\nCDFI certification and USDA RMAP/RBDG eligibility are separate — you do not need to be CDFI-certified to apply for RMAP or RBDG. However, many successful RLF operators hold both:\n\n- CDFI certification strengthens your organizational credibility in USDA applications\n- CDFI Fund FA awards can provide additional RLF capital to layer with RBDG and RMAP\n- CDFI certification demonstrates to USDA reviewers that your organization has been vetted by a federal certifying authority\n\nFor rural organizations, the most common sequence is:\n1. Apply for RBDG grant to establish the RLF\n2. Pursue CDFI certification during the RLF build-out period\n3. Apply for RMAP with your RLF track record\n4. Apply for CDFI Fund FA awards with your RMAP and lending history\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 7 covers the IRP and federal capital stack strategy, including CDFI Fund awards and how to sequence capital sources. Training is independent and not affiliated with the CDFI Fund.*',
  'What Is a CDFI? Community Development Financial Institutions Explained | Cap Fund Academy',
  'CDFIs are Treasury-certified organizations providing financial services to underserved communities. Learn how CDFI certification works and how it relates to USDA RMAP and RBDG programs.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '10 days'
),

(
  'USDA RMAP Application Checklist: 23 Items Reviewers Check First',
  'usda-rmap-application-checklist-reviewers',
  'grant-application-preparation',
  'USDA reviewers check these 23 items when scoring RMAP applications. Most organizations miss at least 5. Use this checklist before you submit.',
  E'## What USDA Reviewers Actually Look For\n\nUSDA RMAP applications are scored at the national level by Rural Development staff applying the 125-point rubric in 7 CFR 4280.316. After reviewing hundreds of applications, certain patterns emerge: the organizations that score competitively have addressed every item on this checklist. Those that miss items lose 5-20 points per gap.\n\n## The 23-Item Pre-Submission Checklist\n\n### Organizational Documents\n☐ Articles of incorporation or tribal charter — current, certified copy\n☐ IRS 501(c)(3) determination letter\n☐ Board of directors list with bios showing relevant experience\n☐ Organizational chart\n☐ Succession plan for key personnel\n☐ Current audited or reviewed financial statements (2-3 years)\n☐ Current fiscal year budget\n\n### Program Documents\n☐ Written loan fund policy — complete, covering all required sections\n☐ 3-year microloan portfolio summary (experienced pathway)\n☐ TA program description with documented outcomes\n☐ Rural service area documentation (USDA ERS RUCA codes or rural designation)\n☐ Staff résumés (loan officer, program director, executive director)\n\n### Compliance Certifications\n☐ SAM.gov active registration with current UEI (check expiration date the day of submission)\n☐ Debarment certification (Form AD-1047) — signed by authorized representative\n☐ Lobbying certification — signed\n☐ Drug-free workplace certification\n☐ Civil rights compliance certifications\n\n### Application Forms\n☐ All USDA RD application forms completed — no blank fields\n☐ All signature lines signed by the authorized organizational representative\n\n### Evidence Exhibits\n☐ All exhibits numbered sequentially and referenced by number in the narrative\n☐ Letters of support/commitment from relevant community partners (target 5-7)\n☐ Environmental screening worksheet completed and in the file\n☐ Application reviewed by at least one person who did not write it\n\n## The 5 Most Commonly Missed Items\n\n**1. SAM.gov expiration** — Expires annually. Check the day before submission.\n\n**2. Succession plan** — Required but frequently absent. A board resolution adopting a written succession policy takes 2-4 weeks to complete.\n\n**3. TA outcome data** — Organizations that provide TA but do not track outcomes cannot demonstrate the connection between TA and loan performance that USDA scores.\n\n**4. Rural borrower documentation** — Claiming rural microloans without USDA-specific rural designation documentation for each borrower''s business address.\n\n**5. Unsigned forms** — Any required signature line left blank creates an intake delay or rejection.\n\n## Using AI to Check Your Score\n\nCap Fund Academy''s AI Scoring tool evaluates your application inputs against the 125-point RMAP rubric and identifies gaps before submission. Available to enrolled students in Cert 12: Application Assembly, Evidence Documentation & AI-Assisted Scoring.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform — not affiliated with USDA.*',
  'USDA RMAP Application Checklist: 23 Items | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Use this 23-item USDA RMAP application checklist before submitting. Covers organizational documents, compliance certifications, evidence exhibits, and the 5 most commonly missed items.',
  true, 'published', now() - interval '1 day'
),

(
  'What Is the RMRF? The Rural Microentrepreneur Revolving Fund Explained',
  'what-is-rmrf-rural-microentrepreneur-revolving-fund',
  'rmap-readiness',
  'The RMRF (Rural Microentrepreneur Revolving Fund) is the restricted account required for all USDA RMAP-approved microlenders. Here is what it is, how it works, and what happens if you use it incorrectly.',
  E'## What Is the RMRF?\n\nThe Rural Microentrepreneur Revolving Fund (RMRF) is the primary restricted account that every USDA RMAP-approved microlender must maintain. USDA requires the RMRF to be held in a separate, interest-bearing bank account that is never commingled with general operating funds or other grant funds.\n\nRegulatory authority: 7 CFR 4280.336\n\n## What Goes Into the RMRF\n\nThe RMRF receives:\n- RMAP grant funds from USDA (the initial capitalization)\n- Principal repayments from RMAP-funded microloans\n- Program income authorized by USDA for deposit (after LLRF requirements are met)\n\n## What Comes Out of the RMRF\n\nThe RMRF may only be used for:\n- Making new RMAP-eligible microloans to rural microenterprises\n\nIt may NOT be used for:\n- Administrative costs\n- TA program expenses\n- General operating expenses\n- Any purpose not specifically authorized by USDA\n\nThis is the most important rule about the RMRF: it is a revolving fund for making microloans only.\n\n## The LLRF: The Required Reserve Account\n\nAlongside the RMRF, RMAP-approved lenders must maintain a separate Loan Loss Reserve Fund (LLRF). The LLRF must equal at least 5% of the total outstanding RMAP-funded microloan principal balance at all times.\n\nThe LLRF:\n- Is held in a separate account from the RMRF\n- Is funded from RMAP grant funds initially and replenished from microloan interest income\n- Is used exclusively to cover losses (charge-offs) on defaulted RMAP microloans\n- Cannot be used for administrative costs\n\n**The 5% rule is checked at every USDA site visit.** Organizations that let the LLRF fall below 5% receive a compliance finding.\n\n## Monthly Reconciliation\n\nBoth the RMRF and LLRF must be reconciled monthly against:\n- The bank statement for each account\n- The outstanding loan schedule\n- The program income ledger\n\nCap Fund Academy Cert 11 covers the complete RMRF/LLRF accounting system, reserve calculator, and monthly reconciliation worksheet.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform — not affiliated with USDA.*',
  'What Is the RMRF? Rural Microentrepreneur Revolving Fund | Cap Fund Academy',
  'The RMRF is the restricted account required for all USDA RMAP microlenders. Learn what goes in, what can come out, the 5% LLRF rule, and monthly reconciliation requirements.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '12 days'
),

(
  'Rural Microloan vs Conventional Bank Loan: Why Rural Entrepreneurs Need Both',
  'rural-microloan-vs-conventional-bank-loan',
  'rural-economic-development',
  'Rural microloans and conventional bank loans serve different borrowers at different stages. Understanding the gap they fill is the foundation of every successful rural microlending program.',
  E'## The Rural Capital Gap\n\nConventional banks have largely exited the small loan market. The minimum profitable loan size for a community bank underwriting process runs $250,000-$500,000. A rural entrepreneur needing $15,000 for equipment to launch a mobile welding business simply cannot get that loan from a bank — the underwriting cost exceeds the interest revenue.\n\nRural microloans exist to fill this gap.\n\n## What Rural Microloans Fund\n\nUnder USDA RMAP, microloans of up to $50,000 can fund:\n- Working capital for rural microenterprises\n- Equipment purchases\n- Inventory\n- Business start-up costs\n- Expansion of existing rural businesses\n\nThe borrower must be a rural microenterprise — a business with 10 or fewer full-time equivalent employees located in a USDA-designated rural area.\n\n## Why Conventional Lenders Cannot Serve This Market\n\n**Loan size economics:** The cost to underwrite a $20,000 loan is nearly the same as a $500,000 loan. At conventional interest rates, a $20,000 loan does not generate enough interest income to cover underwriting and servicing costs.\n\n**Credit profile:** Many rural microentrepreneurs have thin credit files — not bad credit, but limited credit history. Conventional lenders rely on credit scores and cannot accommodate non-traditional credit signals.\n\n**Collateral:** Rural microenterprises often lack the collateral conventional lenders require. A $20,000 loan secured by a food trailer or landscaping equipment may not meet conventional collateral standards.\n\n**Speed:** Rural entrepreneurs sometimes need capital faster than conventional underwriting allows.\n\n## The Credit Elsewhere Requirement\n\nUSDA RMAP microloans require a "credit elsewhere" determination — documentation that the borrower cannot obtain the needed capital from conventional sources at reasonable rates and terms. This is not just a regulatory formality: it confirms that the microloan is actually filling a market gap, not displacing a conventional lender.\n\n## The Graduation Pathway\n\nThe best rural microlending programs create a graduation pathway:\n1. Pre-loan TA builds the borrower''s financial management skills\n2. First microloan ($5,000-$20,000) establishes a documented repayment history\n3. Second microloan builds on the track record\n4. After 2-3 successful microloans, the borrower may qualify for a conventional bank loan\n\nThis is why RMAP requires TA alongside capital — TA is the mechanism that makes graduation possible.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 1 covers microfinance fundamentals and borrower-centered lending in depth.*',
  'Rural Microloan vs Conventional Bank Loan | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Rural microloans fill the capital gap conventional banks cannot serve. Learn why rural entrepreneurs need microloans, what they fund, the credit elsewhere requirement, and the graduation pathway.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '18 days'
),

(
  'How to Write a USDA Rural Business Development Grant (RBDG) Application',
  'how-to-write-rbdg-application',
  'rbdg-readiness',
  'The USDA RBDG application requires a detailed project narrative, budget, leverage documentation, and evidence of rural need. Here is how to write each section to maximize your score.',
  E'## RBDG Application Overview\n\nThe USDA Rural Business Development Grant (RBDG) is a competitive grant program under 7 CFR 4280 Subpart E. Applications are submitted to USDA Rural Development State Offices and reviewed by both state and national USDA staff. Unlike RMAP, RBDG is a grant — no repayment required — which makes it highly competitive.\n\n## Who Can Apply\n\nEligible applicants include:\n- Rural public bodies (counties, municipalities, tribal governments)\n- Private nonprofit organizations\n\nFor-profit businesses are not eligible as the primary applicant.\n\n## RBDG Scoring Criteria\n\nRBDG applications are scored on several factors, including:\n- Leverage: other funds committed alongside the grant\n- Economic need: unemployment rate, poverty rate, and income levels in the service area\n- Population: smaller, more rural communities receive priority\n- Organizational capacity and experience\n- Number of jobs to be created or retained\n- Project impact on rural small businesses\n\n## The Project Narrative\n\nThe RBDG project narrative should address:\n\n**Project description:** What specifically will you do with the grant funds? For an RLF, describe: the fund structure, eligible borrowers, eligible uses, loan terms, governance, and how loans will be made and tracked.\n\n**Need:** Why is this project needed in your specific rural service area? Use Census data, unemployment statistics, and business survey data to document capital access gaps.\n\n**Benefits:** What will be achieved? Jobs created or retained, businesses assisted, capital deployed, and economic multiplier effects.\n\n**Service area:** Describe the rural service area with precision. Include USDA rural designation documentation.\n\n**Leverage:** List every other funding source committed to the project. RBDG rewards high leverage ratios.\n\n**Qualifications:** Describe your organization''s track record with similar programs.\n\n## Budget\n\nThe RBDG budget must:\n- Identify every expense category\n- Show the grant amount and all matching/leveraged funds\n- Be proportional to the scope of work\n- Include only allowable costs (no lobbying, no entertainment, no alcohol)\n\n## Letters of Commitment\n\nLetters of commitment from banks, foundations, and other organizations pledging specific amounts are critical for demonstrating leverage. A letter that says "we support this project" is not a commitment. A letter that says "First National Bank commits $200,000 as a co-investment in this revolving loan fund, contingent on RBDG award" is.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 6 covers the complete RBDG application process, scoring workbook, and budget template.*',
  'How to Write a USDA RBDG Application | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Step-by-step guide to writing a USDA Rural Business Development Grant application: project narrative, scoring criteria, budget, leverage documentation, and letters of commitment.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '20 days'
),

(
  'The Complete Guide to RMAP Technical Assistance Documentation',
  'rmap-technical-assistance-documentation-guide',
  'technical-assistance-entrepreneurs',
  'USDA RMAP requires approved microlenders to document every technical assistance activity. Poor TA documentation costs organizations 10-15 points on their renewal application. Here is the complete documentation system.',
  E'## Why TA Documentation Is Worth 15 Points\n\nThe Technical Assistance program section of the RMAP scoring rubric is worth 15 points — roughly 12% of the total score. Organizations that provide excellent TA but do not document it score significantly lower than those with mediocre TA that is meticulously documented.\n\nUSDA reviewers cannot give credit for activities they cannot verify. Documentation is not a compliance burden — it is the mechanism that converts your work into scoring points.\n\n## What Must Be Documented\n\nFor each TA session, you must record:\n\n**Session log (per session per participant):**\n- Date\n- Duration (hours and minutes)\n- Client name or client ID (privacy-compliant)\n- Session type (1:1 individual, group workshop, virtual, phone)\n- Topics covered\n- Objectives and outcomes\n- Staff member providing TA\n- Whether the client is a current or prospective borrower\n\n**Client-level tracking (across all sessions):**\n- Total TA hours per client\n- Connection between TA and microloan application\n- Business outcomes at 12-month follow-up (revenue, employment, survival)\n\n**Program-level aggregates:**\n- Total TA hours delivered per quarter\n- Total unique clients served\n- Types of TA delivered\n- Percentage of microloan borrowers who received pre-loan TA\n\n## The TA-to-Loan Connection\n\nUSDA RMAP reviewers look specifically for evidence that your TA program improves loan outcomes. The most competitive applications demonstrate:\n- The percentage of borrowers who received TA before their loan\n- The delinquency rate for TA-assisted borrowers vs. non-TA-assisted borrowers\n- Specific business outcomes achieved by TA participants (revenue growth, jobs created, business survival)\n\nIf you are not already tracking these metrics, start now. Retroactive analysis is possible if you have session logs.\n\n## The 25% TA Cap\n\nUnder RMAP, up to 25% of the TA grant award may be used for administrative costs. All remaining TA grant funds must be used for direct TA activities. Exceeding this cap is an audit finding.\n\n## Quarterly TA Report Format\n\nYour quarterly narrative report to USDA should include:\n- Total TA hours delivered in the quarter\n- Types of activities (workshops, 1:1 sessions, distance learning)\n- Number of businesses assisted\n- Notable outcomes and case studies\n- Any challenges and solutions\n- Cumulative year-to-date totals\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 5 provides the complete TA documentation system including session log templates, outcome tracking tools, and quarterly report formats.*',
  'RMAP Technical Assistance Documentation Guide | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Complete guide to USDA RMAP TA documentation: session logs, client tracking, TA-to-loan connection, the 25% cap, and quarterly reporting format. Worth 15 points in your RMAP renewal.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '22 days'
),

(
  'Rural Nonprofit Finance: Understanding Restricted Funds and Revolving Loan Fund Accounting',
  'rural-nonprofit-restricted-fund-accounting-rlf',
  'rlf-accounting-compliance',
  'Revolving loan fund accounting is fundamentally different from general nonprofit accounting. USDA requires strict fund segregation, separate bank accounts, and a program income allocation system most nonprofits have never seen before.',
  E'## Why RLF Accounting Is Different\n\nMost nonprofit accountants are comfortable with fund accounting — tracking restricted and unrestricted funds separately. But revolving loan fund accounting adds layers of complexity that go beyond standard nonprofit accounting practice:\n\n1. **Multiple restricted accounts for a single program** — RMAP requires separate RMRF and LLRF accounts, each with different use restrictions\n2. **Program income rules** — Interest and fees earned on microloans (program income) must be allocated in a specific priority order\n3. **Loan receivable accounting** — Tracking outstanding loan principal as an asset, with an allowance for loan losses\n4. **USDA-specific reporting** — Quarterly narratives, SF-270 drawdown requests, and annual RMRF financial statements\n\n## The Non-Negotiable: Separate Bank Accounts\n\nUSDA does not allow RMAP funds to be commingled with general operating funds or other grant funds — ever. You need:\n\n**Account 1: RMRF Operating Account**\n- Receives RMAP grant funds from USDA\n- Receives microloan principal repayments\n- Used only for making new RMAP-eligible microloans\n- Never used for admin costs or TA expenses\n\n**Account 2: LLRF Reserve Account**\n- Must equal ≥5% of outstanding RMAP microloan principal at all times\n- Funded from RMAP grant funds initially; replenished from interest income\n- Used only to cover losses on defaulted RMAP microloans\n\n**Account 3: General Operating Account**\n- All non-RMAP activities\n- Never receives RMRF or LLRF funds\n\n## Program Income Allocation Priority\n\nInterest income earned on RMAP microloans must be allocated in this order:\n\n1. **First:** Replenish the LLRF to the 5% minimum (if below)\n2. **Second:** With USDA approval, fund allowable administrative costs\n3. **Third:** Deposit to the RMRF for additional re-lending\n\n## The Allowance for Loan Losses\n\nEvery RLF should maintain an Allowance for Loan Losses (ALL) — an accounting estimate of expected credit losses that reduces the gross loan receivable to its net realizable value. This is not a cash account; it is a balance sheet contra-asset.\n\n- **Debit Loan Loss Provision** (income statement expense) to increase the ALL\n- **Debit Allowance for Loan Losses** / **Credit Loans Receivable** when a loan is charged off\n\nThe ALL should reflect your historical loss rate applied to the current outstanding portfolio.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 11 provides the complete RLF accounting system including chart of accounts template, RMRF/LLRF control worksheet, and the LLRF reserve calculator.*',
  'Rural Nonprofit RLF Accounting: Restricted Funds | Cap Fund Academy',
  'RLF accounting requires separate RMRF and LLRF bank accounts, program income allocation in priority order, and an allowance for loan losses. USDA audit-ready accounting explained.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '25 days'
),

(
  'How to Get USDA Funding for a Rural Microloan Program: The Complete 2026 Guide',
  'how-to-get-usda-funding-rural-microloan-program-2026',
  'rmap-readiness',
  'USDA funds rural microloan programs through RMAP (up to $500K), RBDG grants, and IRP loans. Here is the complete 2026 guide to accessing federal capital for your rural lending program.',
  E'## Federal Funding for Rural Microloan Programs in 2026\n\nUSDA Rural Development operates three primary programs that fund rural microloan organizations:\n\n1. **RMAP** — Rural Microentrepreneur Assistance Program: direct loans up to $500,000 to approved MDOs, plus TA grants\n2. **RBDG** — Rural Business Development Grant: competitive grants for RLF establishment and rural business support\n3. **IRP** — Intermediary Relending Program: 1% fixed-rate loans for 30 years to eligible intermediaries\n\n## The Recommended Funding Sequence\n\n**Step 1: Apply for RBDG (Year 1-2)**\nRBDG is the easiest entry point because:\n- It is a grant — no repayment required\n- Eligible applicants include organizations without prior microlending experience\n- Awards typically range from $50,000 to $500,000\n- Use RBDG to establish the RLF and begin making microloans\n\n**Step 2: Apply for RMAP (Year 2-4)**\nAfter demonstrating 1-3 years of microlending history, apply for RMAP:\n- RMAP provides up to $500,000 in revolving loan capital\n- Requires documented microlending experience for competitive scoring\n- Adds a mandatory TA component (funded separately)\n- Quarterly applications — no fixed deadline\n\n**Step 3: Apply for IRP (Year 4+)**\nOnce your RLF has proven operations:\n- IRP provides up to $2,000,000 per application at 1% fixed for 30 years\n- Requires substantial organizational capacity and lending track record\n- Repayment to USDA required quarterly\n- Best for organizations ready to serve larger rural businesses ($50,000-$400,000)\n\n## Key Eligibility Requirements (All Programs)\n\n- Nonprofit, public agency, or tribal entity\n- Rural service area (USDA-designated)\n- Active SAM.gov registration with current UEI\n- No delinquent federal debt\n- Not debarred or suspended\n- Civil rights compliance documentation\n\n## How Competitive Is the Process?\n\n**RMAP:** Highly competitive. Applications are scored nationally on a 125-point rubric. The minimum competitive score varies by quarter based on available funding and number of applicants. Strong applications typically score 90+ out of 125.\n\n**RBDG:** Competitive at the state level. State offices set priorities; rural areas and high-poverty communities receive preference.\n\n**IRP:** Less frequently announced. Watch the USDA Rural Development website for Notice of Solicitation of Applications (NOSA).\n\n## How Cap Fund Academy Helps\n\nOur 17 certifications teach every element of the RMAP, RBDG, and IRP application and compliance process:\n\n- Cert 3: USDA RMAP eligibility, application, and 125-point scoring rubric\n- Cert 6: USDA RBDG application and RLF establishment\n- Cert 7: IRP and federal capital stack strategy\n- Cert 12: Application assembly and AI-assisted scoring\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform. Training does not guarantee USDA funding or eligibility. Consult your USDA Rural Development State Office for program-specific guidance.*',
  'How to Get USDA Funding for a Rural Microloan Program 2026 | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Complete 2026 guide to USDA funding for rural microloan programs: RMAP (up to $500K), RBDG grants, and IRP loans. Eligibility, funding sequence, and how competitive the process is.',
  true, 'published', now() - interval '3 days'
),

(
