  'cap-fund-academy-certifications-pricing',
  'microlending-basics',
  'Cap Fund Academy offers 17 individual certifications ($497-$997 each) and 5 master credential bundles ($1,497-$5,997). Here is exactly what is included in each certification and how to choose the right track.',
  E'## Cap Fund Academy Certification Pricing\n\nCap Fund Academy offers several enrollment options:\n\n### Individual Certifications\n\nEach of the 17 certifications can be purchased individually:\n\n| Certification | Price |\n|--------------|-------|\n| Cert 1: Microfinance Foundations | $497 |\n| Cert 2: RLF Design & Capitalization | $497 |\n| Cert 3: USDA RMAP Eligibility, Application & Scoring | $597 |\n| Cert 4: RMAP Microlender Operations | $497 |\n| Cert 5: RMAP Technical Assistance & Training | $497 |\n| Cert 6: USDA RBDG | $597 |\n| Cert 7: IRP and Federal Capital Stack | $497 |\n| Cert 8: Underwriting, Credit Policy & Portfolio Risk | $597 |\n| Cert 9: Loan Servicing, Collections & Workouts | $497 |\n| Cert 10: Federal Compliance & 2 CFR 200 | $597 |\n| Cert 11: RLF Accounting & Audit Readiness | $597 |\n| Cert 12: Application Assembly & AI-Assisted Scoring | $497 |\n| Cert 13: Community Outreach & Economic Impact | $497 |\n| Cert 14: Automated Offer & Sales Funnel | $497 |\n| Cert 15: Content Engine, Social Media & Ads | $497 |\n| Cert 16: CRM & Business Operations | $497 |\n| Cert 17: Master Capstone | $997 |\n\n### Master Credential Bundles (Save 20-30%)\n\n| Bundle | Includes | Price | Savings |\n|--------|----------|-------|---------|\n| Rural Microfinance Associate | Certs 1-4 | $1,497 | Save $491 |\n| RLF Practitioner | Certs 1-9 | $2,997 | Save $980 |\n| USDA Rural Capital Specialist | Certs 1-13 | $3,997 | Save $1,474 |\n| Certified RLF Executive | Certs 1-15 | $4,997 | Save $1,468 |\n| Master Administrator (All 17) | Certs 1-17 | $5,997 | Save $1,965 |\n\n### Organization License\n$5,000 — All 17 certifications for up to 5 staff members. Includes admin seat management and team progress tracking.\n\n### Done-With-You Application Support\n$10,000 — Full curriculum access plus personalized application review, live coaching sessions, and submission support for your USDA RMAP or RBDG application.\n\n## What Is Included in Every Certification\n\n- Self-paced online lessons (4 modules, 1,500-3,000 words each)\n- AI Study Coach powered by GPT-4o (available 24/7 for questions)\n- Audio lessons in human-quality voice (6 voice options)\n- 15-question quiz (80% passing score)\n- Downloadable templates, checklists, and worksheets\n- Required artifact for practical application\n- Printable certificate upon completion\n- LinkedIn and X/Twitter certificate sharing\n\n## Which Track Is Right for You?\n\n**If you are preparing a USDA RMAP application:** Start with the Rural Microfinance Associate bundle (Certs 1-4) then continue with the USDA Rural Capital Specialist (Certs 1-13).\n\n**If you operate an existing RLF and need compliance help:** Certs 10 (compliance), 11 (accounting), and 12 (application assembly) are the highest priority.\n\n**If you are building a certification business:** Certs 14-17 cover offer design, content marketing, CRM, and the master capstone.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform not affiliated with USDA. See capfundacademy.com for current pricing.*',
  'Cap Fund Academy Certifications: Pricing and What You Get | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Complete pricing guide for Cap Fund Academy certifications: 17 individual certs ($497-$997), 5 master credential bundles ($1,497-$5,997), organization licenses, and done-with-you support.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '5 days'
),

(
  'SAM.gov Registration for USDA Grants: Step-by-Step Guide',
  'sam-gov-registration-usda-grants',
  'grant-application-preparation',
  'Every organization applying for USDA RMAP, RBDG, or IRP must have an active SAM.gov registration with a current UEI. A lapsed registration can delay or kill your application. Here is how to register and maintain it.',
  E'## Why SAM.gov Registration Is Non-Negotiable\n\nSAM.gov (System for Award Management) is the federal government''s primary database for all entities doing business with the government. Without an active SAM.gov registration, your USDA RMAP or RBDG application cannot be processed — regardless of how competitive it is.\n\nThe SAM.gov registration must be active at the time of application submission and must remain active throughout the entire award period.\n\n## The UEI: What Replaced the DUNS Number\n\nIn April 2022, the federal government replaced the DUNS number system with the Unique Entity Identifier (UEI). The UEI is a 12-character alphanumeric identifier assigned by SAM.gov. If your organization was registered in SAM.gov before April 2022, you already have a UEI — it was auto-assigned during the transition.\n\nYour UEI appears in your SAM.gov entity record. Make sure the UEI in your application exactly matches your SAM.gov record.\n\n## Step-by-Step SAM.gov Registration\n\n**Step 1: Gather your information**\n- EIN (Employer Identification Number)\n- Legal organization name exactly as it appears on IRS records\n- Physical address\n- Banking information for electronic payments (required for grant disbursements)\n- NAICS codes relevant to your activities\n\n**Step 2: Create a login.gov account**\nSAM.gov requires login.gov authentication. Create an account at login.gov with your work email.\n\n**Step 3: Register at SAM.gov**\n- Go to sam.gov and log in\n- Click "Register Entity"\n- Complete all required sections\n- Submit — IRS validation typically takes 3-5 business days\n- Full processing can take 2-4 weeks\n\n**Step 4: Track your registration status**\nYou will receive email confirmation when your registration is activated. Note the expiration date — SAM.gov registrations expire exactly 12 months after activation.\n\n## Common SAM.gov Problems That Delay USDA Applications\n\n**Expired registration:** The most common problem. Set a calendar reminder 60 days before your expiration date. Renewal takes 2-4 weeks.\n\n**EIN mismatch:** The EIN in SAM.gov must exactly match your IRS records. Name discrepancies between IRS and SAM.gov slow down validation.\n\n**Wrong entity name:** If your organization changed its legal name, update both IRS records and SAM.gov before applying.\n\n**No banking information:** Grant payments are disbursed via EFT. Your banking information must be complete and current.\n\n## Maintaining Your Registration\n\nRenew annually — set a reminder for 60 days before expiration. Log in to SAM.gov, review your entity information for accuracy, and renew. The renewal process takes 2-4 weeks.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy Cert 10 covers the complete federal certifications required for USDA grant applicants, including SAM.gov/UEI, debarment, lobbying, and drug-free workplace.*',
  'SAM.gov Registration for USDA Grants: Step-by-Step Guide | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Every USDA RMAP, RBDG, and IRP applicant needs active SAM.gov registration with a current UEI. Step-by-step guide, common problems, and maintenance requirements.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '15 days'
),

(
  'How AI Is Changing USDA Grant Application Preparation',
  'ai-usda-grant-application-preparation',
  'grant-application-preparation',
  'AI tools are transforming how rural organizations prepare USDA RMAP and RBDG applications — from evidence gap analysis to narrative strengthening. Here is what AI can and cannot do for your federal application.',
  E'## The AI Revolution in Federal Grant Preparation\n\nArtificial intelligence tools are changing how rural nonprofits and CDFIs prepare USDA program applications. Organizations that understand how to use AI responsibly can identify evidence gaps weeks before submission, strengthen narrative sections, and score their applications against the USDA rubric — all before a single page is submitted.\n\nBut AI tools have real limitations when it comes to federal applications. Using them incorrectly can create false confidence in an application that would not score competitively.\n\n## What AI Can Do Well\n\n**Evidence gap analysis:** AI scoring tools can systematically check whether your narrative addresses each criterion in the USDA rubric. This is faster and more consistent than manual review — and it catches gaps that human reviewers might miss on a first read.\n\n**Narrative feedback:** AI can identify when your narrative uses vague language ("we have extensive experience") rather than specific, verifiable claims ("we originated 47 microloans totaling $1.2M between 2022 and 2024"). This is the metrics-dates-capacity framework that separates competitive applications.\n\n**Checklist verification:** AI can confirm that required elements are present — that your written loan policy covers all required sections, that your exhibits are cited in the narrative, that required certifications are listed.\n\n**Priority ranking:** AI can rank evidence gaps by point value, helping you focus your pre-submission effort on the highest-scoring criteria first.\n\n## What AI Cannot Do\n\n**Verify your actual documents:** AI scoring evaluates what you type into the system — not your actual PDF exhibits. If you claim 80% rural loans but your data shows 62%, AI will not catch the discrepancy unless you enter the accurate number.\n\n**Predict USDA decisions:** USDA makes final determinations based on the complete application package and competitive factors beyond any AI tool''s knowledge. AI scores are estimates, not guarantees.\n\n**Provide legal or compliance advice:** AI tools cannot tell you whether your loan policy meets all regulatory requirements under 7 CFR 4280. That requires human legal review.\n\n**Write your application for you:** AI-generated narrative that is not grounded in your organization''s actual data, actual history, and actual policies will not be competitive.\n\n## Cap Fund Academy''s AI Scoring Tool\n\nCap Fund Academy offers an AI-powered Application Readiness Workspace where enrolled students can:\n- Enter their organizational data and program information\n- Run an AI scoring analysis against the USDA RMAP, RBDG, or IRP rubric\n- Receive a structured report of strengths, weaknesses, missing evidence, and priority fixes\n- Re-score after making corrections to measure improvement\n\nThe tool uses GPT-4o and the scoring rubric stored in the Cap Fund Academy database — which can be updated as regulations change.\n\n**Disclaimer:** Cap Fund Academy''s AI scoring is advisory only and does not constitute a USDA determination of eligibility, score, or fundability. Always verify current scoring criteria at ecfr.gov.\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform — not affiliated with USDA.*',
  'How AI Is Changing USDA Grant Application Preparation | Cap Fund Academy',
  'AI tools are transforming USDA RMAP and RBDG application preparation. What AI can do (evidence gap analysis, narrative feedback) and what it cannot (verify documents, predict USDA decisions).',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '8 days'
),

(
  'Rural Economic Development Certifications: Why Training Matters Before You Apply',
  'rural-economic-development-certifications-why-training-matters',
  'rural-economic-development',
  'Rural organizations that invest in staff training before applying for USDA programs score higher, make fewer compliance errors, and build more sustainable lending programs. Here is the evidence.',
  E'## The Training Gap in Rural Capital Programs\n\nMost rural nonprofits and CDFIs applying for USDA RMAP or RBDG funding do so without comprehensive training in the regulatory requirements. The result: applications that miss evidence in key scoring categories, compliance findings in the first site visit, and loan loss rates higher than they should be.\n\nThis is not a failure of mission. These organizations genuinely want to serve rural microentrepreneurs. The gap is technical knowledge — the kind of knowledge that comes from studying 7 CFR 4280, not from reading a program summary.\n\n## What Untrained Organizations Miss\n\n**On applications:**\n- Written loan policies that lack required sections (automatic point deduction)\n- TA documentation that cannot support the program claims in the narrative\n- Missing rural designation documentation for individual borrowers\n- Exhibits that exist but are not cited in the narrative\n\n**In operations:**\n- LLRF falling below the 5% minimum\n- Program income not allocated in the required priority order\n- TA sessions not logged with required data elements\n- Environmental screening worksheets missing from loan files\n- Adverse action notices not delivered within 30 days of denial decisions\n\nEvery one of these is a USDA audit finding. Most are preventable with training.\n\n## What Training Accomplishes\n\nOrganizations that invest in training before applying:\n\n**Score higher on applications.** When staff understand the scoring rubric, they can design their program — and document it — to maximize points. Training teaches you that loan committee meeting frequency is scored; regular meetings start happening before the application is submitted.\n\n**Make fewer compliance errors.** Knowledge of 2 CFR 200, civil rights requirements, and environmental review procedures prevents the audit findings that consume management time and create risk.\n\n**Build more sustainable programs.** Understanding of underwriting principles, portfolio risk management, and workout procedures means lower default rates and a healthier revolving fund.\n\n**Operate with less staff turnover risk.** Organizations with documented, trained knowledge are less vulnerable to the loss of a key staff member who "knows where everything is."\n\n## What Cap Fund Academy Teaches\n\nCap Fund Academy''s 17 certifications are built by practitioners who have read the actual regulations — not summaries. Our content cites specific CFR provisions. Our quizzes test regulatory precision, not general familiarity.\n\nThe result is a trained practitioner who can:\n- Explain why the LLRF must equal 5% of outstanding RMAP principal (and what the regulation actually says)\n- Identify which loan purposes are ineligible under RMAP and why\n- Write a narrative that cites exhibits by number and uses the metrics-dates-capacity framework\n- Run a monthly RMRF/LLRF reconciliation without external assistance\n\n*Cap Fund Academy is an independent training platform. Training does not guarantee USDA funding or compliance outcomes.*',
  'Rural Economic Development Certifications: Why Training Matters | Cap Fund Academy',
  'Rural organizations with trained staff score higher on USDA applications and make fewer compliance errors. Why training before applying for RMAP and RBDG produces better outcomes.',
  false, 'published', now() - interval '30 days'
)

